.TH CLIPMUNGE 1 "2026-09-03" "clipmunge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clipmunge \- rule\-driven Wayland clipboard rewriter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clipmunge
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clipmunge
watches the Wayland clipboard and rewrites what it finds there according to
rules you write in Lua. A rule can leave the plain text alone while attaching
a link to the
.B text/html
flavour, or replace a URL with a version that has had its tracking parameters
removed.
.PP
The thing it can do that other clipboard tools cannot is serve
.B different bytes for different MIME types of the same selection.
.BR wl-copy (1)
hands out the same buffer for every type it advertises, so it cannot make a
selection whose plain text is a bare ticket number and whose HTML is a link to
that ticket. clipmunge speaks the
.B ext-data-control-v1
protocol directly, where a data source answers each request separately.
.PP
It does nothing at all until you give it rules. There is no built\-in
behaviour and no default rule set: a clipboard daemon that starts rewriting
things you did not ask it to rewrite is a bad neighbour. Without a config file
it prints the path it looked at and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Load \fIPATH\fR instead of the default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipmunge/config.lua .
Symlinks are followed on every load, and both ends of the link are watched.
See RELOADING.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Load the config, report anything wrong with it, and exit without connecting to
the compositor. Patterns are compiled here, so a bad regular expression is
reported now rather than on some unlucky copy later.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reload
Do not watch the config directory for changes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-notify
Never run the notify command, whatever the rules ask for.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Log every selection that arrives and every rewrite, before and after.
.IP
.B This writes clipboard contents to the log in full.
Everything you copy while it is on ends up in the journal, including whatever
a password manager puts there. It is a switch for working on a rule, not a
setting to leave on. Without it the log records MIME types and byte counts
only, never content.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary, including the config path this machine would use, and
exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH RELOADING
The directory holding the config is watched, and a change reloads the rules
within about 150 milliseconds. The
.I directory
rather than the file, because editors do not write in place: vim writes a
temporary file and renames it over the target, which leaves a watch on the
original inode looking at a file nobody will touch again. Watching the
directory also picks up changes to anything the config
.BR require s
from a neighbouring file.
.PP
When the config is a symlink, both directories are watched: the one holding
the name you gave, and the one holding the file it resolves to. A dotfiles
repository is edited at the far end of the link; a config manager such as
home\-manager or stow instead publishes a new file and moves the link, and the
event then happens at the near end. A candidate directory on a read\-only
filesystem is skipped, with a line saying so \- a file that cannot be edited
in place cannot be worth watching, and on NixOS the far end is
.I /nix/store
whose traffic has nothing to do with your rules.
.PP
The config is resolved afresh on every load rather than once at start\-up, so
a swapped symlink is followed to the new file rather than pinning the daemon
to the one it happened to start with.
.PP
A config that fails to load does not disarm the daemon. The error is logged
and the rules that were already working stay in place, so a typo cannot
silently switch your clipboard back to plain.
.SH SECURITY
The config file is trusted, in the same way a shell startup file is. It can
set
.B notify_command
with
.BR clipmunge.settings ,
and that runs a program with rule output as one of its arguments. That is what
the setting is for, and it is also why a rule set somebody sent you is not
something to install unread.
.PP
A
.I rule
gets much less.
.BR io ,
.BR os ,
.BR dofile ,
.BR load ,
.B coroutine
and the C module loader are never loaded into the interpreter \- not removed
afterwards, never loaded \- and
.B package.path
points at the config directory alone. At copy time a rule can compute and
nothing else: it cannot open a file, reach the network, or start a process.
.PP
The difference between \(lqnever loaded\(rq and \(lqremoved\(rq is the whole
guarantee. Setting a global to
.B nil
leaves the library in
.BR package.loaded ,
which is the first place
.B require
looks, so
.B require(\(dqos\(dq).execute
finds it again. Not loading it leaves nothing to find.
.PP
Handlers run on a budget \- roughly ten million VM instructions and 64 MB of
Lua heap, thousands of times what a real rule needs. It is a guard against
your own endless loop, not against an adversary: a rule that overruns is
logged and skipped, and the clipboard keeps working.
.PP
A selection whose owner advertises
.B x-kde-passwordManagerHint
is left alone entirely: not rewritten, not read, and so not logged even under
.BR \-\-debug .
The check runs on the announced flavour list before a byte is fetched, because
after that the content is already in the process. The list is configurable \-
see
.B secret_mimes
in
.BR clipmunge (5)
\- and it is a courtesy that depends on the other application bothering to
send the hint, which most do not.
.PP
The one thing a rule can send outwards is the text of a notification, and even
that it only describes; the daemon decides whether to send it, truncates it to
200 characters, and sends at most one per rewrite. The notify command is a
list of arguments, never a shell string, so clipboard content substituted into
it stays an argument no matter what punctuation it contains.
.B \-\-no\-notify
switches the mechanism off entirely.
.PP
.B \-\-debug
suspends the part of this that concerns the log. See above.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
The compositor to connect to. Required.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config lives, and only when it is set to an
.I absolute
path; empty or relative falls back to
.IR ~/.config ,
as the basedir specification requires. Taking a relative value literally would
resolve the config against the service's working directory.
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by systemd for a
.B Type=notify
unit.
.B clipmunge
sends
.B READY=1
to it once the config has loaded and the compositor has answered, so the unit
is not considered started before rewrites can happen. Ignored when unset.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Overrides the log level, e.g.
.IR RUST_LOG=debug .
Note that this changes what is logged, not what is collected: contents are
only gathered with
.BR \-\-debug .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non\-zero when the config is missing or unusable, when the compositor does not
implement
.BR ext-data-control-v1 ,
or when the data device is taken away. A rule that throws an error is logged
and skipped; it does not stop the daemon.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipmunge/config.lua
Rules and settings. See
.BR clipmunge (5).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clipmunge (5),
.BR wl-copy (1),
.BR wl-paste (1),
.BR notify-send (1)
